// Title: Potential Application Whitelisting Bypass via Dnx.EXE
// Sigma ID: 81ebd28b-9607-4478-bf06-974ed9d53ed7
// Status: test | Level: medium
// Author: Beyu Denis, oscd.community
// Date: 2019-10-26 | Modified: 2024-04-24
// ATT&CK: stealth, T1218, T1027.004
// Description: Detects the execution of Dnx.EXE. The Dnx utility allows for the execution of C# code. Attackers
//   might abuse this in order to bypass application whitelisting.
// False positives:
//   - Legitimate use of dnx.exe by legitimate user
// References:
//   - https://lolbas-project.github.io/lolbas/OtherMSBinaries/Csi/
//   - https://enigma0x3.net/2016/11/17/bypassing-application-whitelisting-by-using-dnx-exe/
// Sigma rule: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/blob/05d1031f537e830b76da53bae2400c5814c89165/rules/windows/process_creation/proc_creation_win_dnx_execute_csharp_code.yml
// License: Detection Rule License (DRL) 1.1 - https://github.com/SigmaHQ/Detection-Rule-License
// Converted by sigma2kql (target: microsoft_xdr, pySigma 1.5.1, pySigma-backend-kusto 1.0.1)
// Validation: passed (Kusto.Language 12.4.1, microsoft_xdr schema)

DeviceProcessEvents
| where FolderPath endswith "\\dnx.exe"
